{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 New York Mets were a team that spent most of the year teasing their fans with potential before ultimately falling short of the postseason. They opened the season as one of the most hyped rosters in baseball, with a new ownership group, a blockbuster signing in Francisco Lindor, and genuine World Series aspirations. April was a bumpy 10-11 start, but moments like Michael Conforto's walk-off single on April 11th — plating two runs to steal a win — hinted at the drama that would define the year. The team looked like genuine contenders through May, going 17-9 and leaning on clutch hits and bullpen double plays to grind out victories.\n\nJune brought the Mets back to earth at 15-15, a month defined by bullpen misfires and the terrifying vulnerability of Edwin Díaz in high-leverage situations. Trevor May and Díaz combined to blow a game on June 1st in gut-wrenching fashion, and Jacob Barnes surrendered a Fernando Tatis Jr. grand slam that encapsulated the team's fragility against elite competition. Yet Francisco Lindor's clutch single late in June was a reminder of what this roster could be. July was a rollercoaster at 14-13 — José Peraza provided unlikely heroics, Conforto and McCann launched timely home runs, but Díaz's grand slam implosion against Pittsburgh on July 17th was the signature wound of the season, a -0.820 WPA nightmare that summarized the bullpen's boom-or-bust nature.\n\nAugust is where the wheels truly came off. An 11-19 record exposed the Mets as a flawed team incapable of sustaining momentum. Pete Alonso and Kevin Pillar provided bright spots, but the team was bleeding wins faster than they could manufacture them. September confirmed the collapse, with the bullpen — Díaz, Lugo, and Familia — continuing to implode in crucial moments. Back-to-back blown saves against Washington in early September and a crushing Jeurys Familia surrender late in the month ensured the Mets would finish well below .500. What began as a pennant race ended as a 77-85 cautionary tale about the gap between expectation and execution.\n\nIn the end, 2021 was a season haunted by what might have been. The offense was inconsistent despite 176 home runs, and the bullpen — Díaz in particular — cost the team multiple winnable games at the worst possible moments. The Mets finished eight games under .500, missing the playoffs and leaving a fanbase that had dared to dream wondering how a roster with this much talent could so thoroughly underperform. It set the stage for a winter of rebuilding, soul-searching, and the inevitable question: would this core ever live up to its billing?",
